# How Google Finds and Ranks Websites

Have you ever searched for something on Google and wondered how it decides which websites appear first?

You type a few words into the search box, press Enter, and within seconds, Google presents millions of possible results.

Some websites appear at the top. Others are buried several pages down. And some don't appear at all.

But how does Google know these websites exist? How does it understand what each page is about? And more importantly, how does it decide which websites deserve to appear first?

It isn't magic. There's a process behind it.

Understanding this process can help you make better decisions about your website, create more useful content, and improve your chances of appearing in relevant search results.

Let's break it down.

## 1. How Does Google Find Websites?

Google doesn't manually visit every website on the internet.

Instead, it uses automated software programs called crawlers to discover publicly accessible web pages.

One of Google's main crawlers is called Googlebot.

Think of Googlebot as a visitor that moves from one web page to another, following links and discovering new content along the way.

For example:

1. Google discovers a link to your website.
1. It visits that URL.
1. It finds links to other pages on your website.
1. It may follow those links to discover additional pages.
1. It can return later to check for updated content.

This process is known as **crawling**.

### Where does Google find website links?

Google can discover URLs through several methods, including:

- Links from other websites.
- Internal links between pages on your website.
- XML sitemaps submitted through Google Search Console.
- Previously discovered URLs that Google revisits.

This is one reason why having a well-structured website matters.

If your important pages are difficult to reach or have no links pointing to them, Google may struggle to discover them.

### Does Google find every website automatically?

No.

Google is constantly discovering new pages, but that doesn't mean every page on the internet will be found or crawled.

A new website with no links pointing to it may take longer to be discovered.

You can help by setting up Google Search Console, submitting an XML sitemap, and making sure your important pages are accessible.

If you've just launched your website, read our guide on [How to Get Your New Website on Google](https://emmystack01.com/articles/how-to-get-your-new-website-on-google).

## 2. What Happens When Google Crawls a Website?

When Googlebot discovers a URL, it may attempt to crawl the page.

During crawling, Google retrieves the page's content and resources so it can understand what the page contains.

This includes things such as:

- Text and headings.
- Images and image information.
- Links to other pages.
- Page titles and descriptions.
- Structured data.
- Other content and resources available to the crawler.

Google can also process JavaScript to understand content that is rendered dynamically.

However, websites with technical problems can make crawling more difficult.

For example:

- Your server may return an error.
- Important pages may be blocked from crawling.
- The website may load very slowly or inconsistently.
- Important content may not be accessible to Google.
- Too many unnecessary URLs may consume crawling resources.

This is why website development and technical SEO are connected.

A website shouldn't only look good to human visitors. Its technical structure should also allow search engines to access and understand its important content.

## 3. How Does Google Index Websites?

Discovering and crawling a page doesn't automatically mean Google will show it in search results.

After crawling, Google processes the page and may add it to its search index.

This process is called **indexing**.

Think of Google's index as a massive library containing information about web pages that Google has processed and chosen to include.

When someone searches for something, Google can retrieve relevant pages from this index.

### What does Google consider during indexing?

Google tries to understand what a page is about.

It may consider information such as:

- The words and topics on the page.
- Page titles and headings.
- Images and other media.
- Internal and external links.
- Canonical URLs.
- Structured data.
- The overall content and context.

Google may also decide not to index a page.

For example, a page might not be indexed because:

- It contains a noindex instruction.
- Google cannot access or process it properly.
- It is a duplicate of another page.
- It redirects to another URL.
- Google has not discovered or crawled it yet.
- Google determines that it should not be included in the index.

Being crawled is not the same as being indexed.

And being indexed does not guarantee that a page will appear for every search query.

## 4. How Does Google Decide Which Website Ranks First?

This is where things get interesting.

Imagine two businesses offering the same service.

Both have websites. Both publish information about their services. Both want to appear when potential customers search for what they offer.

Why might one appear above the other?

Google's ranking systems evaluate many signals to determine which results are most relevant and useful for a particular search.

There isn't one magic ranking factor that guarantees the top position.

Here are some important areas to understand.

### 1. Relevance to the Search Query

Google tries to understand what someone is searching for and find pages that match that intention.

For example, someone searching for:

"Affordable website designer in Lagos"

probably wants to find a website designer who serves Lagos and offers affordable services.

A page that clearly explains website design services, its target audience, location, and relevant details may be more suitable than a page that only talks generally about technology.

This is why understanding your audience's search terms matters.

Your website should use language that reflects what your potential customers actually search for.

### 2. Content Quality and Usefulness

Google aims to provide helpful and reliable information.

A page that genuinely answers a visitor's question is generally more useful than one filled with repetitive keywords and unnecessary text.

For example, imagine a photographer creating a page about wedding photography.

One website simply says:

"We offer the best wedding photography services. Contact us today."

Another explains its photography style, services, packages, location, booking process, and includes examples of previous work.

The second page gives visitors more useful information to make a decision.

Don't create content just to fill your website. Create content that serves a purpose.

### 3. Website Experience

Visitors should be able to use your website without struggling.

Consider these questions:

- Does the website work properly on mobile devices?
- Can visitors find important information easily?
- Does the page load without unnecessary delays?
- Are buttons and navigation links working?
- Is the content easy to read?
- Is the website secure?

Google uses page experience signals, including Core Web Vitals, as part of its ranking systems.

However, having perfect performance scores does not guarantee a top position.

The goal should be to create a website that works well for real people, not just to chase technical scores.

### 4. Links and Website Authority

Links help search engines discover pages and understand relationships between websites.

They can also provide signals that help Google's systems assess the relevance and prominence of pages.

There are two important types of links to understand.

**Internal links**

These connect pages within your own website.

For example, an article about website design can link to your website development services page.

Internal links help visitors navigate your website and help search engines discover related pages.

**External backlinks**

These are links from other websites pointing to yours.

For example, a business directory, industry publication, or relevant blog might link to your website.

Relevant, trustworthy links can help establish your website's reputation and prominence.

However, buying large numbers of random links or participating in manipulative link schemes can violate Google's spam policies.

Focus on earning relevant links through useful content, genuine partnerships, and valuable work.

### 5. Search Intent

Search intent refers to what someone actually wants to accomplish when they search.

Consider these searches:

- "What is website design?"
- "How much does a website cost in Nigeria?"
- "Website designers in Lagos"
- "Hire a website developer"

Although they all relate to websites, the people searching have different intentions.

The first person wants to learn.

The second is researching prices.

The third is looking for a local service provider.

The fourth may be ready to hire someone.

Your content should match the purpose behind the search.

A service page is more appropriate for someone looking to hire a developer, while an educational article is more appropriate for someone trying to understand website design.

### 6. Location and Personalisation

Some searches have a local intention.

For example:

"Restaurants near me"

"Law firms in Lagos"

"Website designers in Nigeria"

For these searches, Google may consider the searcher's location and other contextual information when selecting results.

Search results can also vary based on factors such as language, device, and the specific query.

This means two people searching for the same phrase may not always see identical results.

If your business serves a specific location, make that clear on your website and maintain accurate business information across relevant platforms.

## 5. Does Google Rank the Entire Website or Individual Pages?

This is an important distinction.

Google generally ranks individual pages for specific search queries, rather than assigning one fixed position to an entire website.

For example, a business website might have:

- A homepage targeting its main brand and services.
- A web design page targeting website design searches.
- A pricing page targeting cost-related searches.
- Blog articles answering specific customer questions.

Different pages can appear for different searches.

Your homepage might not rank first for every service you offer, but a dedicated service page or helpful article may be more relevant to a particular search.

This is why creating useful, focused pages is important.

Don't try to make one page answer every possible question.

Give important topics the space they deserve.

## 6. How Can You Help Your Website Rank Better on Google?

Now that you understand how Google discovers and evaluates websites, what can you actually do to improve your website's visibility?

Here are practical steps to start with.

### 1. Research What Your Customers Search For

Don't guess what people are searching for.

Think about the questions potential customers ask before choosing a business like yours.

For example, a website development company might target topics such as:

- How much does a website cost in Nigeria?
- Do small businesses need websites?
- How long does it take to build a website?
- What should a business website have?

These topics can become useful articles and website pages.

### 2. Create Dedicated Pages for Important Services

If your business offers multiple services, don't squeeze everything into one page.

Create clear pages for your main services.

For example:

- Website Design
- Branding
- UI/UX Design
- SEO Services

Each page should explain what the service involves, who it is for, the benefits, and how interested customers can get started.

### 3. Write Helpful, Original Content

Publish content that answers real questions.

Avoid copying other websites or creating dozens of nearly identical pages just to target different keywords.

A smaller collection of genuinely useful pages can be more valuable than a large collection of repetitive content.

### 4. Improve Your Website's Technical Foundation

Make sure your website:

- Uses HTTPS.
- Loads important pages correctly.
- Works on mobile devices.
- Has a logical URL structure.
- Uses appropriate canonical URLs.
- Doesn't accidentally block important pages from indexing.
- Has a working XML sitemap.
- Uses clear internal links.

Technical SEO helps search engines access and understand your website.

### 5. Improve Your Internal Linking

Connect related pages naturally.

For example, an article explaining SEO can link to your SEO service page.

An article about website costs can link to your pricing page.

This creates a useful connection between educational content and the services your business provides.

### 6. Build Your Website's Reputation

Create work worth referencing.

Publish useful resources, showcase projects, collaborate with relevant businesses, and build genuine relationships within your industry.

Don't treat backlinks as a numbers game.

Relevant references from trustworthy sources are more valuable than meaningless links from unrelated websites.

### 7. Monitor Your Performance

Use Google Search Console to understand how your website performs in Google Search.

Pay attention to:

- Search queries bringing impressions.
- Pages receiving clicks.
- Average search positions.
- Indexing problems.
- Changes in search visibility.

Use the information to identify opportunities and improve your website over time.

SEO is not a one-time activity. It requires monitoring, learning, and making improvements.

## 7. How Long Does It Take to Rank on Google?

There is no fixed timeline for ranking a website.

A new website may get indexed relatively quickly but take much longer to gain meaningful search visibility.

The timeline depends on several factors, including:

- How competitive the search terms are.
- The quality and relevance of your content.
- Your website's technical condition.
- Your website's existing reputation.
- The strength of competing websites.
- How well your pages satisfy search intent.

For example, ranking for your business name may be easier than ranking for a broad phrase such as "best website designer."

These are very different levels of competition.

Be careful with anyone promising guaranteed first-page rankings within a specific number of days.

No legitimate SEO provider can guarantee a particular organic search position.

The better approach is to build a strong foundation, publish useful content, and improve consistently.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### 1. Does Google rank websites automatically?

Yes. Google's automated ranking systems evaluate eligible pages and determine which results are most relevant to individual search queries.

Website owners cannot directly choose their organic ranking positions.

### 2. Can a new website rank on Google?

Yes. New websites can appear in Google Search and rank for relevant queries.

However, building search visibility can take time, especially when competing against established websites.

### 3. Does posting articles regularly improve Google rankings?

Publishing articles regularly does not automatically improve rankings.

What matters is whether the content provides genuine value, addresses relevant topics, and serves your audience.

Publishing low-quality articles simply to maintain a schedule is unlikely to produce meaningful results.

### 4. Do keywords guarantee higher Google rankings?

No. Keywords help you understand and communicate what a page is about, but repeating them excessively does not guarantee better rankings.

Use relevant phrases naturally and focus on answering the visitor's question.

### 5. Can social media followers improve Google rankings?

Having a large social media following does not directly guarantee better organic search rankings.

However, social media can help people discover your content, visit your website, and share useful resources that may attract relevant links and attention.

### 6. Can I pay Google to rank my website first?

You can pay for Google Ads to appear in sponsored placements, subject to Google's advertising systems and policies.

However, advertising does not buy you a higher organic search position.

Organic SEO and paid advertising are separate.

## Final Thoughts

Google doesn't simply choose websites at random.

It discovers pages through crawling, processes information through indexing, and uses automated ranking systems to select relevant results for each search.

Understanding these three stages gives you a clearer picture of what your website needs.

First, make sure Google can discover your website.

Next, make sure your important pages are accessible and eligible for indexing.

Then focus on creating useful content, improving the website experience, building a strong reputation, and making your pages relevant to the searches your audience performs.

You don't need to understand every technical detail of Google's algorithms to get started.

You need a website that works properly, communicates clearly, and gives people a good reason to visit.

